COMMAND NAME: gpscp

Copies a file from the local host to multiple hosts at once.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpscp { -f <host_file> | - h <hostname> [-h <hostname> ...] } 
      [-p <scp_port>] [-u <os_user>] [-v] <local_file> <remote_path>

gpscp -?


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpscp utility allows you to copy a file from the local host to 
a specified location on multiple hosts at once using SCP (secure copy).

To specify the hosts involved in the SCP session, use the -f option to 
specify a file containing a list of host names, or use the -h option to 
name single host names on the command-line. At least one host name (-h) 
or a host file (-f) is required.

If a user name is not specified in the host list file or with the 
-u option, gpscp will copy files as the currently logged in user. To 
determine the currently logged in user, do a whoami command. By default, 
gpscp goes to $HOME of the session user on the remote hosts after login. 
To ensure the file is copied to the correct location on the remote hosts, 
it is recommended that you use absolute paths.

The gpscp utility requires Python 2.3 or higher. Python is installed 
with most operating systems by default. If you have an older version of 
Python, you must upgrade to 2.3 or higher (and make sure Python is in 
your $PATH) in order to use gpssh.

Before using gpscp, you must have a trusted host setup between the hosts 
involved in the SCP session. You can use the utility gpssh-exkeys to 
update the known host files and exchange public keys between hosts if 
you have not done so already.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-f <host_file>

Specifies the name of a file that contains a list of hosts that will 
participate in this SCP session. The host name is required, and you 
can optionally specify an alternate user name and/or SCP port number 
per host. The syntax of the host file is one host per line as follows:
[username@]hostname[:scp_port]

-h <hostname>

Specifies a single host name that will participate in this SCP 
session. You can use the -h option multiple times to specify 
multiple host names.

-p <scp_port>

Specifies the default SCP port to use when logging in to a remote 
host. If not specified, defaults to port 22 (or the port specified 
in the host file, if any).

-u <os_user>

Specifies the default operating system user account to use when 
copying a file to a remote host. If not specified, defaults to the 
currently logged in user (or the user specified in the host file, if any).

-v (verbose mode)

Optional. Reports additional messages.

<local_file>

Required. The file name (or absolute path) of a file on the local 
host that you want to copy over to the remote hosts.

<remote_path>

Required. The path where you want the file to be copied on the 
remote hosts. If an absolute path is not used, the file will be 
copied relative to $HOME of the session user.

-? (help)

Displays the online help.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Copy the file named installer.tar to / on all the hosts in the 
file host_file.

gpscp -f host_file installer.tar /


Copy the file named myfuncs.so to the specified location on 
the hosts named seg1 and seg2:

gpscp -h seg1 -h seg2 myfuncs.so /usr/local/greenplum-db/gp-pgsql-2.3.1.0/lib


